    "question": {
      ":question/id": "378e41b2-4aeb-59bc-a361-22ed956e395c",
      ":question/problem": "Find $q$, if $2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $q$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+q\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n-4q=8\\,⟹\\,q=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $q$ such that $2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2}=0.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
