      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $q$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+q\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n2q=-2\\,⟹\\,q=-1.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $q$ such that $2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2}=0.$",
